---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Licensing"
  description="The OpenE2EE Signal Protocol SDK is available under AGPLv3, or under a commercial license for proprietary products."
>
  <header class="page-hero">
    <div class="container measure">
      <p class="label">Licensing</p>
      <h1>One SDK. Two ways to ship it.</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        The same code either way. What differs is the obligations you take on — and this page is a
        summary of them, not the license itself.
      </p>
    </div>
  </header>

  <section class="band">
    <div class="container">
      <dl class="definition-rows">
        <div>
          <dt>AGPLv3</dt>
          <dd>
            The complete SDK, at no cost, for products whose distribution and network use can meet
            the AGPLv3 obligations. Those obligations are real: AGPLv3 reaches applications offered
            over a network, not only distributed binaries. Read the license text in the repository
            before you rely on this path — it controls, not this description.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>Commercial license</dt>
          <dd>
            A separately negotiated production grant for proprietary products, so AGPLv3 does not
            apply to the covered product. Scoped to a named legal entity, named covered products,
            and an explicit licensed-component list.
          </dd>
        </div>
      </dl>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band band-surface">
    <div class="container measure prose">
      <h2>The agreement is the license</h2>
      <p>
        Everything on this website describes the intended commercial model in plain language. It
        does not replace the agreement, and it is not legal advice. Where the two differ, the
        agreement controls the grant, the covered products, the support commitments, renewal, and
        what happens after expiration.
      </p>
      <p>
        Which agreement depends on the path. Startup is self-service, so the agreement is the
        versioned <a href="/legal/terms">commercial terms</a> you accept in checkout, and the
        version you accepted is the one that keeps applying. Growth and Enterprise are scoped first
        and closed on a signed order form.
      </p>
      <p>
        If you are unsure which path applies to your product, ask before you build. It is a shorter
        conversation than the one after launch.
      </p>
      <p class="text-links">
        <a href="/legal/terms">Review Startup terms</a>
        <a href="/pricing">See pricing</a>
        <a href="mailto:licensing@open-e2ee.dev?subject=Commercial%20licensing%20question">
          Ask a licensing question
        </a>
        <a href="https://github.com/open-e2ee/signal-protocol-js/blob/main/LICENSE">
          Read the license text
        </a>
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
